.vscode/**
.vscode-test/**
src/**
node_modules/**
# ...except exactly the production runtime closure of `out/extension.js`
# (verified with `npm ls --omit=dev` plus reading its compiled requires):
# vscode-languageclient pulls vscode-languageserver-protocol
# (which pulls vscode-jsonrpc), plus minimatch/brace-expansion/
# balanced-match. Where npm puts those three is npm's business and it has
# already moved once: the roots now hold vsce's *dev* copies, and the
# production ones sit nested under vscode-languageclient/node_modules/,
# which the first negation below already covers. The root negations stay
# for the day npm hoists them back. Without them the packaged .vsix fails to load the
# language client on a fresh install, because VS Code resolves the entry's
# `require("vscode-languageclient/node")` inside the extracted folder
# alone. Everything else under node_modules stays excluded: the toolchain
# (vsce, typescript, every `@types/*`) and the dev-time dep forest.
# Adding a new production dependency means adding one line here; forgetting
# to breaks first activation of a freshly installed extension, the silent
# kind, so watch for `Cannot find module` errors in the extension host log.
!node_modules/vscode-languageclient/**
!node_modules/vscode-languageserver-protocol/**
!node_modules/vscode-jsonrpc/**
!node_modules/minimatch/**
!node_modules/semver/**
!node_modules/brace-expansion/**
!node_modules/balanced-match/**
.gitignore
tsconfig.json
**/*.ts
**/*.map
